bhmsvd","bhkdm","bgui","bgtsl","bgs","bgigjy","bfq","bfow","bfb","bexls","beuok","bephxe","bekjur","bedb","bdj","bdb","bdbdm","bcyze","bcvcx","bculiw","bclgay","bbitdv","bay","azy","aznis","azmuhv","azbv","ayvdd","ayqi","ayovs","ayl","ayks","ayjj","aygq","ayejhb","aydrp","aycx","ayao","axxtvx","axjg","avy","avvv","avqj","avmxcu","aul","attg","atk","asq","asq","asmi","ashsk","asfwmm","asblnh","arqzn","arpynv","arjya","ari","aqzuqb","aqwjp","aquzwc","aquc","aqnoac","aqlul","apogdy","apm","aplct","api","aowbf","aora","aolhn","aoi","aofzw","anys","anvkk","ansh","amwih","amr","amkbc","amj","amjsp","aminbp","amh","alyhm","alva","ali","aldaa","ajtaf","ajph","ajivfx","aja","aipykd","ahypo","ahwg","ahtw","ahta","ahjd","ahctcl","agz","agyn","agwxvp","agv","agsofv","agolp","aglkxm","agh","afqx","afo","afd","aew","aebq","adtpre","adny","adkeq","adhfqj","acznyn","acpq","acoar","acgtj","acdr","accxc","abvwhd","abitwe","abfhbw","aaxnz","aavlu","aar","aahqfa","aafo","aae","aaezev"]},"output":["zyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba"]}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def alienOrder(self, words: List[str]) -> str:\n        graph = {c: set() for w in words for c in w}\n        indeg = {c: 0 for c in graph}\n        for a, b in zip(words, words[1:]):\n            for x, y in zip(a, b):\n                if x != y:\n                    if y not in graph[x]:\n                        graph[x].add(y)\n                        indeg[y] += 1\n                    break\n            else:\n                if len(a) > len(b):\n                    return ''  # longer word before its own prefix\n        queue = deque(c for c in graph if indeg[c] == 0)\n        order = []\n        while queue:\n            c = queue.popleft()\n            order.append(c)\n            for d in graph[c]:\n                indeg[d] -= 1\n                if indeg[d] == 0:\n                    queue.append(d)\n        return ''.join(order) if len(order) == len(graph) else ''\n","approach":"**Each adjacent pair of words yields at most one ordering constraint, and the answer is a topological sort of those constraints.** Compare `words[i]` and `words[i+1]` character by character; the first mismatch `x != y` means `x` precedes `y`. Later characters say nothing. If there is no mismatch and the first word is longer, the input is invalid.\n\nBuild a directed graph over the letters that occur (every letter is a node even if it has no edges), then run **Kahn's algorithm**: enqueue in-degree-0 letters, pop them in turn, decrement the in-degree of their successors. If the output has fewer letters than the graph, a cycle exists and we return `\"\"`.\n\n**Why it works:** the constraints are exactly the information the sorted list provides; any linear extension of the resulting DAG is consistent with the input, and a cycle means the input is self-contradictory.\n\n**Common mistakes:** comparing non-adjacent words (redundant, but fine) while missing the prefix rule; adding duplicate edges and double-counting in-degree; dropping letters that only appear in one word with no relations; forgetting that only the first difference counts.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** How would you produce the lexicographically smallest valid order? (Replace the queue with a min-heap.) How do you detect the cycle with DFS instead of Kahn? (Three-colour marking.) How many constraints can there be at most? (At most `len(words) - 1`, so the graph is tiny.)","complexity":{"time":"O(total characters + 26^2)","space":"O(26^2)"},"similar":["course-schedule-ii","sequence-reconstruction","verifying-an-alien-dictionary"],"number":117,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def alienOrder(self, words: List[str]) -> str:\n        pass\n"}